0", "note_text": "Horana Plantations PLC's Q1 FY2026/27 revenue was essentially flat YoY at Rs.933.1Mn (-0.3%), but gross profit rose 14.6% to Rs.182.0Mn on a mix shift within the segmental note: Tea segment revenue rose 2.2% (Rs.719.0Mn to Rs.734.9Mn) while Tea's segmental gross profit jumped 29.2% (Rs.101.6Mn to Rs.131.3Mn), the single largest driver of the quarter's earnings growth. This was partly offset by the Rubber segment, where revenue fell 24.9% (Rs.87.0Mn to Rs.65.3Mn) and the segmental gross LOSS widened from Rs.(9.2)Mn to Rs.(21.9)Mn. Oil Palm was broadly stable (revenue -3.0% to Rs.102.9Mn, gross profit -4.9% to Rs.59.1Mn). Below the operating line, finance expenses rose 30.6% to Rs.49.5Mn as short-term interest-bearing borrowings grew to Rs.1,264.1Mn from Rs.752.5Mn a year earlier, funding working capital; finance income also rose (Rs.0.7Mn to Rs.2.1Mn) but on a small base. Administrative expenses grew 11.1% to Rs.93.0Mn. Net result: PBT +6.6% to Rs.58.4Mn, and PAT +14.2% to Rs.39.0Mn (EPS Rs.1.56 vs Rs.1.37), aided by a 6.0% lower tax charge.", "source_doc_id": "52191", "source_page": 3, "gaps_note": "No QoQ comparison possible: no quarter closer to this one has been collected for HOPL -- this is the first financial_statements entry for this symbol. YoY figures use only the filing's own 30 June 2025 comparative column.", "takeaway": "The Tea segment's sharp margin recovery (+29.2% gross profit) drove essentially all of the quarter's earnings growth -- revenue itself was flat. Without Tea's improvement, the widening Rubber segment loss and 30.6% higher finance costs (from a Rs.500Mn+ increase in short-term borrowings) would have largely offset the gain.", "qoq": [], "yoy": [{"metric": "revenue", "label": "Revenue", "prior_period_label": "Q1 FY2025/26 (quarter ended 30 June 2025)", "prior_value": 935515, "new_period_label": "Q1 FY2026/27 (quarter ended 30 June 2026)", "new_value": 933126, "pct_change": -0.0025536736449976753, "direction": "deteriorated